| FR-030 | `get_unread_counts` is not executable by the `anon` role; authenticated use keeps working. | Must | AC-031 |
| FR-031 | After a page reload, if the drawer was open, it reopens on the same conversation for the same user on the 
same browser; if that conversation is no longer accessible, the conversation list opens without an error; a closed 
drawer stays closed. | Must | AC-032 |
| FR-032 | Existing messaging behavior (Phase 4 flows: receipts, attachments, voice notes, stars, DM/group 
notifications and their once-across-tabs claim, failed-send retry for attachment messages, reconnect catch-up) does 
not regress. | Must | AC-033 |
| FR-033 | Degraded mode when durable storage is unavailable: the composer is not cleared until the server confirms 
the message; a visible notice says the browser cannot keep unsent messages; leaving the page while a send is 
unconfirmed triggers the browser's leave-page warning; on failure the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo" 
apply. | Must | AC-039 |
| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations of a message (outbox item, create and fence responses, 
feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and the feed matches pending items to stored messages only 
by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in every sender tab, including identical texts and Realtime 
arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |

## 8. Business rules

| ID | Rule | Rationale |
|---|---|---|
| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, 
fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender 
other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
| BR-002 | Only text messages and replies without attachments/voice are queued. | R1-q4 A |
| BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order with at most one create request in progress across all 
tabs; a failed (definitive or expired) item blocks later items of that conversation until the user retries or deletes 
it. Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E; review r3 #5 |
| BR-004 | Each item gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt reuses it (except the 
`CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` retry, FR-007); the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`), and a fenced attempt can never commit. | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B; review r3 #1 |
| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client session 
is valid â†’ retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 5xx 
â†’ fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected â†’ hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT`, 404, 400, 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 422, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch â†’ 
failed, no automatic retry. | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #2, #11 |
| BR-006 | Expiry: an item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send becomes "expirada" and is never sent 
automatically; a manual "Tentar de novo" sends it with the same key. | R1-q5 D, R2-q3 B |
| BR-007 | Cancel only for items known not to be stored, with no earlier attempt able to commit, and not claimed; a 
cancelled item is removed in all tabs and never dispatched. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A; reviews r2 #2, #5, r3 #1 |
| BR-008 | An explicit user sign-out with unsent items requires confirmation; confirming deletes that user's outbox; a 
sign-out not initiated by the user keeps it. Cancelling has no side effect. | R2-q5 A, R2-q6 A |
| BR-009 | Typing: only current conversation members join and send on a conversation's typing topic; a user's own 
typing (any session) is never shown to them; an indicator expires â‰¤ 5 s after the last signal; residual exposure to 
a tampered removed-member client is bounded by access-token lifetime (D3). | R1-q8 A, D, E; R1-q16 E; D3 |
| BR-010 | Mentions: only current members of the conversation at first creation, other than the sender, can be 
mentioned (rooms: members of the room conversation); no `@todos`/`@here`. | R1-q10 A, E; R2-q10 A; D4 |
| BR-011 | Desktop-notification eligibility (extends Phase 4): toggle on and permission granted; never for the user's 
own message; the same user must still be signed in and a member at emission time; never while any tab of the user in 
that browser is visible and viewing that conversation; DM/group: every new message; room: only messages that mention 
the user; at most one notification per message per browser. | R1-q10 B, R1-q16 C/F, R2-q8 A, R2-q9 Aâ€“C; reviews r2 
#9, r3 #3 |
| BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 A, 
R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
| BR-013 | Metric log lines contain only closed-enum event type and outcome/reason, bounded durations/counts, 
conversation type, timestamp, and server-derived app version â€” never message text, attachment names, conversation 
names, user names or emails, user/conversation identifiers, or any client-supplied free text. | R1-q12 B, R2-q2, 
confirmation point 9; review r2 #14 |
| BR-014 | Database rollout: migrations stay local during the phase (written + applied locally with recorded rollback 
SQL); at phase end they are applied to `vhabpcoyypobgasacsko` only with explicit owner authorization, followed by 
catalog readback and a runtime smoke check; the application is deployed by the owner after the migrations; rollback is 
app first, then migrations. | R1-q13 A |
| BR-015 | Tests: only integration (real local Supabase/Realtime/API routes) and E2E (real UI, Playwright); no new 
unit tests; a touched unit test is replaced by integration/E2E coverage and deleted, recorded in TRACK. Each new E2E 
spec counts only after 3 consecutive passing runs. | AGENTS.md test policy, R2-q13 A |
| BR-016 | Conversation restore is per browser and per user; a different user never inherits it. | R2-q11 A, B, D |

## 9. Data and integrations

### Data inputs
- Composer text, reply target, and mention selections (user IDs).
- Browser connectivity events, Realtime stream status, tab visibility, Supabase auth state changes.
- Responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status and error code) and from the fence/lookup call.

### Data outputs
- Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, 
requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / failed + reason / expired), 
attempt number, claim holder and lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across users.
- Database: attempt-fencing state per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced attempt cannot commit; the 
original submitted composition used for replay comparison; mention records written atomically with the message; 
private typing-topic authorization (RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = auth.uid()::text` â†’ `users.id` 
membership); `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE; a 
separate rate-limit bucket for replays and fence/lookup calls.
- API: create route sender and attempt fencing, winning-insert quota charging, `clientMessageId` in responses; 
fence/lookup route for the caller's own message only; authenticated, rate-limited, size-bounded metrics route.
- Structured metric log lines (BR-013).
- Persisted drawer state per user: open flag + active conversation ID.

### Integrations
- Supabase Postgres, Realtime (`postgres_changes`, private broadcast topics), Auth (server routes use 
`createSupabaseServerClient` + `auth.getUser()`).
- Browser APIs: IndexedDB, Web Locks and/or BroadcastChannel for cross-tab claims and signals, Notification API (Phase 
4), Page Visibility, `beforeunload` (degraded mode).
- Hosting/Supabase logs as the metrics sink; no new external analytics dependency.

## 10. Constraints and assumptions

### Constraints
- Next.js App Router, TypeScript strict, Supabase, TanStack Query; reuse existing hooks, repositories, routes, and 
query keys rather than parallel implementations.
- Identity: RLS compares `users.supabase_uid` with `auth.uid()::text`; foreign keys use `users.id`; messages link to 
rooms through `conversations.room_id`.
- Browsers: Chrome and Edge desktop (automated E2E); Firefox desktop (verified). Safari and mobile not required.
- No service worker; background notifications are page-level only.
- No message text, names, or identifiers in metrics (BR-013); no external analytics service.
- The sign-out confirmation must not change the Presence logout sequence (presence-safety rules apply to that touch 
point).
- Online database changes only at phase end with explicit authorization (BR-014); the hosted app currently runs the 
pre-Phase-4 build until the owner deploys, so new server contracts must stay compatible with that build until 
deployment.
- Repository test policy (BR-015); type-check, lint, and build must pass.
- Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: 
claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch â‰¤ 50 events / 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s 
metrics abort.

### Assumptions
- None beyond the user-confirmed answers and owner decisions recorded in `state.md`.

## 11. Edge cases and failure modes

| Case | Expected behavior |
|---|---|
| Tab closed right after pressing send, before server confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load it is 
fenced and delivered once (AC-040). |
| Request stored but response lost | Item becomes unknown; fence finds it stored; resolves as delivered; never offered 
for cancel or expired (AC-038, AC-042). |
| Original create blocked before its INSERT while another tab fences | The fence wins; the blocked attempt can no 
longer commit; the item is resent once or cancelled/expired without a late duplicate (AC-045). |
| Two tabs come online together | One drain claim per conversation; only the oldest unresolved item is eligible; no 
overlapping create requests for that conversation; each stored and shown once, in order (AC-005). |
| Owning tab closes or hangs mid-request | Closed or lease expired: another tab fences and finishes once; while the 
lease is live no other tab dispatches (AC-041). |
| Cancel races a claim | Exactly one wins; a cancelled item is never dispatched; a dispatched item cannot be cancelled 
(AC-006). |
| Lost access before reconnect | First item fails definitively with text; later items of that conversation wait; other 
conversations deliver (AC-007). |
| Access regained after failure | "Tentar de novo" delivers it, then the waiting items in order (AC-008). |
| Other definitive outcomes (404, 400, 413, 422) and 5xx | Definitive â†’ failed with text; 5xx â†’ unknown â†’ fence 
(AC-046). |
| `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` | Failed; "Tentar de novo" resends the content under a new key, once (AC-043). |
| Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests | Items stay pending, retried automatically; quota 
charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate budget (AC-009). |
| Laptop asleep > 24 h | Not-stored items become "expirada"; stored ones resolve as delivered (AC-010, AC-042). |
| Offline attachment / voice | Upload failure: per-file error + retry; create failure after upload: Phase 4 composer 
failure; nothing in the outbox (AC-011). |
| IndexedDB unavailable | Degraded mode: composer kept until confirmed, notice, leave-page warning; no silent loss 
(AC-039). |
| Identical texts sent twice; Realtime arrives before the HTTP response | One bubble per message, matched by key 
(AC-050). |
| Account switched while a drain is paused or a response is in flight | Drain stops; no dispatch as the new user; late 
responses discarded; server rejects any request whose sender differs (AC-044, AC-047). |
| Session ends without Sair; other user signs in | Other user never sees/sends A's items; A's items drain after A 
signs in (AC-012). |
| Temporary Auth failure (401 with valid client session) | Treated as transient; retried; not held or deleted 
(AC-012). |
| Sair with unsent items | Dialog; Cancelar has no side effects at any entry point; Sair mesmo stops drains everywhere 
and deletes the outbox; late responses do not resurrect items (AC-013, AC-047). |
| Realtime drop < 5 s | No "Reconectandoâ€¦" banner (AC-014). |
| Only the conversation-list stream fails | Banner after 5 s and polling after 30 s follow its health; recovered 
independently (AC-015, AC-016). |
| Stale poll returns after a newer Realtime update | Newer/enriched state kept; no duplicates (AC-048). |
| Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s (AC-016). |
| Typist closes the tab or leaves the conversation mid-typing | Indicator disappears â‰¤ 5 s (AC-017). |
| Anon, outsider, public-topic, or removed-member typing access | Refused / no events; removed member's app leaves at 
once; tampered client bounded by D3 (AC-019). |
| Forged mention IDs, plain "@name", changed mentions on replay, recipient removed before replay | No mention for 
non-members/self/plain text; changed composition â†’ 409; unchanged replay returns the stored message (AC-024, 
AC-046). |
| Recipient removed or account switched after commit but before the notification is shown | No notification (AC-049). |
| Hidden tab receives a mention event first while another tab views that conversation | No notification (AC-026). |
| Malicious, oversized, hanging, or sustained metrics traffic | Rejected or dropped; route rate-limited; messaging 
unaffected (AC-028, AC-029). |
| Restored conversation no longer accessible | Conversation list opens without error (AC-032). |

## 12. Security, privacy, compliance, and abuse considerations
- Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to the application user ID, fenced against account switches 
on the client and on the server (expected-sender check on create and fence), and deleted on confirmed explicit 
sign-out.
- The fence/lookup call acts only on the caller's own message for that key, never creates a message, and is rejected 
for anon users and for other users' keys.
- Replays and fence/lookup calls have their own per-user budget so the create-quota exemption (D1) cannot be used for 
unlimited traffic.
- Typing topics are private and members-only for join and send (Realtime RLS with `users.supabase_uid = 
auth.uid()::text` â†’ `users.id`); residual exposure to tampered removed-member clients accepted (D3).
- Mentions are validated and stored server-side in the message transaction; clients cannot write them directly; only 
members can read them; notifications are re-authorized at emission time.
- Notifications never leak content of conversations the user is not (or no longer) a member of.
- Metrics: closed enums, bounded numbers and sizes, server-derived version, per-user rate limit, no free text 
(BR-013); unauthenticated requests are rejected and produce no metric log line.
- `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
- No service-role key in client code; service-role use never replaces application authorization checks.
- Every migration, RLS/Realtime policy, repository, and API route change passes the Supabase/RLS review gate.

## 13. Accessibility, localization, and usability considerations
- UI copy in Brazilian Portuguese. The banner text, sign-out dialog text, typing phrases, and mention notification 
title in this SPEC are required; item-state labels ("pendente", "enviando", "expirada"), actions ("Cancelar", "Tentar 
de novo", "Apagar"), and the degraded-mode notice are proposed, and equivalent Portuguese wording with the same 
meaning is accepted (D4).
- Connection banner uses a polite live region; item states have text labels, not only icons.
- Item actions and the sign-out dialog are keyboard-operable with visible focus; the dialog traps focus and Esc means 
Cancelar.
- The mention list follows the combobox/listbox pattern and is announced to screen readers.
- The mention highlight does not rely on color alone.

## 14. Acceptance criteria

- AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows it 
as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
- AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X with quota available, when 
connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the 
original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
- AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network error 
after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
- AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads while offline, then the browser's offline error is 
shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. 
Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app later delivers it once.
- AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in conversation X 
in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously and m1's real create request 
is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until m1 is resolved; the network logs of both tabs show no 
overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, exactly once each; the database holds one row per key.
- AC-006 (FR-005, BR-007): Given a waiting item never dispatched, when A cancels it in tab 2 at the moment tab 1 tries 
to claim it, then either the cancel wins (the item disappears from both tabs and no create request for it is ever 
sent) or the claim wins (no cancel action is offered and the item is delivered once); given a dispatched item, it 
shows "enviando" and offers no cancel.
- AC-007 (FR-007, BR-003, BR-005): Given A queued m1 and m2 in room conversation X and m3 in DM Y, and A lost access 
to X before reconnecting, when connectivity returns, then m1 shows failed with its text and "Tentar de novo" / 
"Apagar", m2 stays waiting with no create request sent, and m3 is delivered.
- AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, then m1 
and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 is 
attempted next.
- AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, then 
rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real route: 
a replay of an already-stored message and a fence/lookup call do not reduce the remaining create quota but are limited 
by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge the create quota once; concurrent distinct keys at the 
quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
- AC-010 (FR-009, BR-006): Given a never-dispatched item whose send time is more than 24 h ago, when connectivity 
returns, then no create request is sent for it and it shows "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" / "Apagar"; "Tentar de 
novo" delivers it once.
- AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a file, then its upload shows the existing per-file error 
and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; given A's uploads succeeded and then the network drops, 
when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing enters 
the outbox.
- AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has unsent items and A's browser session ends 
without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token revoked so the client reports signed out), when C signs in 
on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no create or fence request is sent while C is authenticated; 
when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as A. Given a 401 while A's client session is valid, then the 
item stays pending and is retried.
- AC-013 (FR-013, BR-008): Given A has 2 unsent items, when A clicks Sair from each in-app entry point, then the 
dialog shows "VocÃª tem 2 mensagens nÃ£o enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens."; Cancelar sends no logout request, 
shows no notice, and does not navigate; Sair mesmo with a drain in flight in another tab stops all drains, deletes the 
outbox, and signs out with the Presence logout sequence as before; after A signs in again the outbox is empty. Given 0 
unsent items, Sair signs out without a dialog.
- AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexÃ£o â€” mensagens serÃ£o 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages 
stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectandoâ€¦" 
appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user action.
- AC-015 (FR-015): Given each required stream is dropped independently (messages stream only; conversation-list stream 
only, including a terminal closure; typing stream only), when Realtime becomes available again, then that stream 
recovers without user action; B's messages sent during the outage appear once; unread counts are correct; typing works 
again; recorded recovery events show increasing, capped retry delays.
- AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, when 
B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started appears 
within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update. Given only the conversation-list stream is unhealthy 
for more than 30 s, polling starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when it becomes visible, 
a fetch happens immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message delivered by both appears 
once, and no new polling request starts during the next 60 s.
- AC-017 (FR-017; must-not R1-q16 E): Given B types continuously for 15 s, then A sees the typing indicator at every 1 
s sample; when B stops, it disappears within 5 s; when B closes the tab mid-typing, within 5 s; when B switches to 
another conversation mid-typing, within 5 s.
- AC-018 (FR-018): Given a group or room with A, B, C, D, when B types, A sees "<B> estÃ¡ digitandoâ€¦"; B and C â†’ 
"<B> e <C> estÃ£o digitandoâ€¦"; B, C, D â†’ "3 pessoas estÃ£o digitandoâ€¦"; a direct conversation shows the 
single-typist phrase.
- AC-019 (FR-019, BR-009): With real local Realtime: an anon client, an authenticated non-member, and a client on the 
public topic name receive no typing events of conversation X and cannot send typing events members receive; member A 
receives B's typing; when member E is removed from X, E's app leaves the topic and shows/receives no further typing 
