9:The Virtual Office messaging drawer (`MessagingDrawer` → `MessageFeed` → `MessageComposer` / `MessageItem`, state in 
`MessagingContext`, data in `useMessages` / `useConversations`, Realtime in `useMessageSubscription` / 
`useConversationRealtime`, typing in `useConversationPresence`) was completed for timeline features in Phase 4. Phase 
4 also delivered a failed send that keeps text/reply/files with a single-message retry (`clientMessageId` idempotency 
in `POST /api/messages/create`, stored in `messages.client_message_id`), a Realtime catch-up after reconnect, and 
opt-in desktop notifications for DM/group messages. This phase adds: a durable per-user outbox for text messages with 
server-fenced attempts and safe cross-tab and cross-account behavior; a connection banner; application-level recovery 
and health for every messaging Realtime stream with polling fallback; a reliable, named, members-only typing 
indicator; live cross-device unread/read sync; @mentions with desktop notifications (including rooms); messaging 
health metrics as redacted server logs; and two Phase 4 database follow-ups plus restoring the open conversation after 
reload.
13:Workspace members using the chat on unstable networks or sleeping laptops lose work and trust: a send that fails 
must be retried by hand, a reload or closed tab discards it (the idempotency key lives only in component memory), and 
nothing tells the user the chat is disconnected. Optimistic messages are matched to stored ones by guessing from 
content and time. Realtime recovery is incomplete: the SDK retries errored/timed-out channels, but the application 
only tracks and catches up the messages channel, has no recovery for terminal closures of the conversation-list 
channel, and has no fallback, so new messages and unread counts can silently stop updating. The typing indicator 
disappears after ~4 s of continuous typing and is broadcast on a public channel. Reading on one device leaves stale 
unread badges on the user's other devices. Nobody can mention someone, so room conversations (which never notify) 
cannot reach a specific person. Nobody can tell in production whether messaging is healthy.
22:- G5: Unread/read state and own sent messages stay in sync across the same user's tabs and devices (≤ 5 s while 
connectivity is healthy).
29:- With Realtime down and HTTP working, other members' new messages appear within 45 s of the outage start, and 
within 15 s (plus request time) of being sent once polling is active.
30:- With healthy connectivity and Realtime, cross-device/tab sync of unread counts, read state, and own sent messages 
within 5 s (D4); during a Realtime outage the polling deadlines above apply.
51:- Durable outbox (IndexedDB, per application user, per browser) for text messages and replies, written before 
dispatch; item states; cancel; per-conversation ordered automatic drain across tabs; claim leases and recovery; 
server-fenced attempts; identity fencing; rejection, rate-limit, and 24 h expiry handling; session-end and sign-out 
rules; degraded mode when durable storage is unavailable.
52:- Server support on create: sender fencing; attempt fencing; key-based correlation (`clientMessageId` exposed to 
the sender in every message representation); quota charged only for a winning insertion with a separate budget for 
replays and fence/lookup calls; mentions persisted atomically with the message and compared against the original 
composition on replay; a fence/lookup call by `clientMessageId` that never creates a message.
55:- Polling fallback after > 30 s of a required stream outage, while the tab is visible.
60:- Phase 4 follow-ups: membership check inside `mark_conversation_read`; `get_unread_counts` not executable by 
`anon`; restore the open conversation after page reload.
63:- Loading the application while offline (service worker / offline app shell) (D2).
78:- J2 Reload/close: A has pending messages and reloads while offline (the browser shows its normal offline error) or 
closes the tab. When the page loads again with internet, the pending messages are there and are sent.
83:- J7 Realtime outage: after 5 s the banner shows "Reconectando…"; after 30 s the open conversation and the list 
update every 15 s; when Realtime returns, polling stops and the banner disappears.
93:| FR-001 | Every eligible send (text and/or reply, no attachments/voice; online or offline) is first written 
durably to the current user's outbox with its `clientMessageId`, conversation, content, reply target, and requested 
mention IDs, and only then dispatched and the composer cleared. If the durable write fails, the send runs in degraded 
mode (FR-033). The item shows in the feed as pending while not confirmed by the server. | Must | AC-001, AC-003, 
AC-040 |
95:| FR-003 | The outbox survives reload and tab close. Reloading while offline shows the browser's normal offline 
error; when the app loads again, the pending items are shown and sent. Items of the current user appear in every tab 
of the same browser. | Must | AC-004 |
96:| FR-004 | Cross-tab ordering and ownership: the outbox order is shared by all tabs; per conversation, only the 
oldest unresolved item is eligible, and at most one tab holds the conversation's drain claim at a time (atomic claim). 
A claim is a lease: it ends when its tab closes or crashes, or when its holder exceeds the attempt deadline (request 
timeout 30 s; lease 45 s), and another tab then resumes after fencing (FR-006). Across all tabs, no two create 
requests of the same conversation's outbox are in progress at once. | Must | AC-005, AC-041 |
97:| FR-005 | Cancel: an item can be cancelled from any tab only if it is known not to be stored and no earlier 
attempt can still commit (never dispatched, last attempt definitively rejected, or fenced as not stored) and it is not 
claimed; the cancel is an atomic transition against claims — a cancelled item is never dispatched afterwards; a 
dispatched item shows "enviando" without cancel. | Must | AC-006 |
98:| FR-006 | Attempt fencing: each dispatch is an attempt of the item's `clientMessageId`. When an attempt ends 
without a usable response (network error, timeout, tab closed, lease expired, HTTP 5xx), the item is unknown. Before 
any cancel, expiry, takeover, or new dispatch, the app calls a server fence/lookup by `clientMessageId` that never 
creates a message and atomically either returns the stored message (item resolves as delivered) or guarantees that no 
earlier attempt for that key can commit afterwards (item returns to waiting). | Must | AC-038, AC-042, AC-045 |
99:| FR-007 | A definitive rejection (BR-005) stops automatic attempts for that item; it shows as failed with its text 
and actions "Tentar de novo" and "Apagar"; later items of the same conversation wait until it is resolved; other 
conversations are unaffected. For `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, "Tentar de novo" sends the same content under a new 
`clientMessageId`. | Must | AC-007, AC-008, AC-043, AC-046 |
100:| FR-008 | A rate-limit rejection is transient: the item stays pending and is retried automatically, preserving 
order and without duplicates. The create quota is charged only for a winning insertion (including among concurrent 
requests for the same key); replays that return an already-stored message and fence/lookup calls do not consume the 
create quota but consume a separate per-user budget, and exceeding that budget is a transient rate limit. | Must | 
AC-009 |
101:| FR-009 | An item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send is never sent automatically: once fenced as not 
stored, it shows as failed "expirada" with "Tentar de novo" (manual send, same key) and "Apagar"; an unknown item 
found stored resolves as delivered, never as expired. | Must | AC-010, AC-042 |
102:| FR-010 | Attachments/voice are never queued. A file whose upload fails offline keeps the existing per-file error 
and retry (send stays disabled until uploads finish); a message whose uploads succeeded but whose create request fails 
keeps the Phase 4 composer failure and "Tentar de novo". | Must | AC-011 |
103:| FR-011 | Identity fencing: each item belongs to an application user (`users.id`). Before every dispatch and 
fence call the app checks that the authenticated user equals the item's owner; every dispatch carries the expected 
sender, and the create and fence routes reject (without storing or fencing) a request whose authenticated user 
differs. When the authenticated user changes or signs out in any tab, all drains in all tabs stop; create, fence, and 
lookup responses that arrive afterwards are discarded: they never add content to another user's view, never resurrect 
deleted items, and never change a newer claim. | Must | AC-012, AC-044, AC-047 |
105:| FR-013 | When the user clicks Sair at any in-app sign-out entry point with N > 0 unsent items, a dialog shows 
"Você tem N mensagens não enviadas. Sair apaga essas mensagens." with "Sair mesmo" and "Cancelar". Cancelar produces 
no logout request, success notice, or navigation. Sair mesmo stops drains in all tabs, deletes that user's outbox, and 
runs the existing sign-out (including the Presence logout sequence unchanged). With N = 0 sign-out is unchanged. | 
Must | AC-013 |
106:| FR-014 | Banner: "Sem conexão — mensagens serão enviadas quando voltar" as soon as the browser reports offline; 
"Reconectando…" when any required messaging Realtime stream (messages/receipts/reactions, conversation list) has been 
unhealthy for more than 5 s while the browser is online; it disappears by itself once all are healthy. | Must | AC-014 
|
108:| FR-016 | Polling fallback: while any required stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, the app fetches the open conversation's new messages and the conversation list every 15 s; on becoming 
visible while still unhealthy it fetches immediately, then every 15 s; no polling while hidden; once all streams are 
healthy no new polling request starts. Merging polling and Realtime results never duplicates a message and never 
replaces newer or enriched state (attachments, mentions, reactions, receipts, loaded history pages) with older data. | 
Must | AC-016, AC-048 |
111:| FR-019 | Typing signals travel only on a private Realtime topic whose join and send are authorized for current 
conversation members; anon clients, authenticated non-members, and public-topic attempts never receive them; the app 
removes a member's client from the topic as soon as it learns of the removal. Payload: user identity and typing flag 
only. Residual risk accepted (D3): a tampered client of a removed member may receive typing signals until its access 
token expires. | Must | AC-019 |
113:| FR-021 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, within 5 s and without reload, focus change, or reconnect: 
when the user reads messages (fully or partly) on one tab/device, their other tabs/devices show the same unread counts 
and read state, and the sender's receipt summary/details agree with the stored receipts on all of the sender's 
sessions. During an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-021 |
114:| FR-022 | While connectivity and Realtime are healthy, a message the user sends from one device/tab appears on 
their other devices/tabs within 5 s without reload; during an outage, FR-016 deadlines apply. | Must | AC-022 |
116:| FR-024 | On first creation the server stores mentions in the same transaction as the message, as application 
user IDs of conversation members at that time other than the sender; other requested IDs create no mention. The 
original submitted composition (content, reply target, requested mention IDs) is what replays are compared against: an 
unchanged replay returns the stored message without re-validating recipients; a different composition under the same 
key returns `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`. Clients cannot write mention records directly; only conversation members can 
read them. | Must | AC-024, AC-046 |
122:| FR-030 | `get_unread_counts` is not executable by the `anon` role; authenticated use keeps working. | Must | 
AC-031 |
126:| FR-034 | Key-based correlation: the sender's representations of a message (outbox item, create and fence 
responses, feed fetches, Realtime events) carry its `clientMessageId`, and the feed matches pending items to stored 
messages only by that key, so each message shows as exactly one bubble in every sender tab, including identical texts 
and Realtime arriving before the HTTP response. | Must | AC-050 |
132:| BR-001 | The outbox belongs to one application user (`users.id`) on one browser; it is never shown, dispatched, 
fenced, or deleted while another user is authenticated, and the server never stores or fences an item under a sender 
other than its owner. | R1-q3 A, R1-q16 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #6, r3 #4 |
134:| BR-003 | Per conversation, items are sent in creation order with at most one create request in progress across 
all tabs; a failed (definitive or expired) item blocks later items of that conversation until the user retries or 
deletes it. Conversations are independent. | R1-q5 A, R1-q2 E; review r3 #5 |
135:| BR-004 | Each item gets a stable `clientMessageId` when the user presses send; every attempt reuses it (except 
the `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED` retry, FR-007); the server stores at most one message per (sender, conversation, 
`clientMessageId`), and a fenced attempt can never commit. | R1-q2 B, R1-q16 B; review r3 #1 |
136:| BR-005 | Outcome classes: transient = browser offline before dispatch, HTTP 429, HTTP 401 while the client 
session is valid → retry automatically; unknown = network error/timeout/no response after dispatch, lease expiry, HTTP 
5xx → fence (FR-006); session-ended = client auth reports signed out / refresh rejected → hold (FR-012); definitive = 
403 `NOT_PARTICIPANT`, 404, 400, 413 `CONTENT_TOO_LARGE`, 422, 409 `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, sender mismatch → 
failed, no automatic retry. | R1-q5 C, R2-q4 A, R2-q5 A; reviews r2 #2, #11 |
137:| BR-006 | Expiry: an item not stored 24 h after the user pressed send becomes "expirada" and is never sent 
automatically; a manual "Tentar de novo" sends it with the same key. | R1-q5 D, R2-q3 B |
138:| BR-007 | Cancel only for items known not to be stored, with no earlier attempt able to commit, and not claimed; 
a cancelled item is removed in all tabs and never dispatched. | R1-q5 B, R2-q7 A; reviews r2 #2, #5, r3 #1 |
143:| BR-012 | Polling runs only while a required Realtime stream has been unhealthy for more than 30 s and the tab is 
visible, every 15 s (immediately on becoming visible), and no new poll starts once all streams are healthy. | R1-q7 A, 
R1-q16 D, R2-q12 C |
154:- Responses from `POST /api/messages/create` (status and error code) and from the fence/lookup call.
157:- Browser IndexedDB outbox keyed by application user ID: `clientMessageId`, conversation ID, text, reply target, 
requested mention user IDs, pressed-send time, order, state (waiting / claimed / unknown / failed + reason / expired), 
attempt number, claim holder and lease expiry. Deleted per BR-007/BR-008; never shared across users.
158:- Database: attempt-fencing state per (sender, conversation, `clientMessageId`) so a fenced attempt cannot commit; 
the original submitted composition used for replay comparison; mention records written atomically with the message; 
private typing-topic authorization (RLS on `realtime.messages`, `users.supabase_uid = auth.uid()::text` → `users.id` 
membership); `mark_conversation_read` with an internal membership check; `get_unread_counts` without `anon` EXECUTE; a 
separate rate-limit bucket for replays and fence/lookup calls.
159:- API: create route sender and attempt fencing, winning-insert quota charging, `clientMessageId` in responses; 
fence/lookup route for the caller's own message only; authenticated, rate-limited, size-bounded metrics route.
179:- Timing parameters chosen under the owner's delegation (D4) and adjustable in planning only with owner approval: 
claim request timeout 30 s, lease 45 s, metrics batch ≤ 50 events / 16 KB, one metrics delivery in flight, 5 s metrics 
abort.
188:| Tab closed right after pressing send, before server confirmation | Item is already in the outbox; on next load 
it is fenced and delivered once (AC-040). |
189:| Request stored but response lost | Item becomes unknown; fence finds it stored; resolves as delivered; never 
offered for cancel or expired (AC-038, AC-042). |
190:| Original create blocked before its INSERT while another tab fences | The fence wins; the blocked attempt can no 
longer commit; the item is resent once or cancelled/expired without a late duplicate (AC-045). |
192:| Owning tab closes or hangs mid-request | Closed or lease expired: another tab fences and finishes once; while 
the lease is live no other tab dispatches (AC-041). |
196:| Other definitive outcomes (404, 400, 413, 422) and 5xx | Definitive → failed with text; 5xx → unknown → fence 
(AC-046). |
198:| Server rate limit during drain; concurrent same-key requests | Items stay pending, retried automatically; quota 
charged once per stored message; replays/fences use a separate budget (AC-009). |
208:| Only the conversation-list stream fails | Banner after 5 s and polling after 30 s follow its health; recovered 
independently (AC-015, AC-016). |
210:| Realtime down > 30 s, tab hidden | No polling; on visible, fetch immediately then every 15 s (AC-016). |
213:| Forged mention IDs, plain "@name", changed mentions on replay, recipient removed before replay | No mention for 
non-members/self/plain text; changed composition → 409; unchanged replay returns the stored message (AC-024, AC-046). |
220:- Outbox content lives only in the user's browser, scoped to the application user ID, fenced against account 
switches on the client and on the server (expected-sender check on create and fence), and deleted on confirmed 
explicit sign-out.
221:- The fence/lookup call acts only on the caller's own message for that key, never creates a message, and is 
rejected for anon users and for other users' keys.
222:- Replays and fence/lookup calls have their own per-user budget so the create-quota exemption (D1) cannot be used 
for unlimited traffic.
227:- `mark_conversation_read` membership check and `anon` revoke on `get_unread_counts` close two Phase 4 findings.
240:- AC-001 (FR-001, BR-002): Given A's browser context is offline, when A sends a text message, then A's feed shows 
it as pending with a text label, the composer is empty, the outbox holds it, and B does not see it.
241:- AC-002 (FR-002, BR-003, BR-004): Given A queued 3 messages offline in conversation X with quota available, when 
connectivity returns, then within 10 s and without any click B sees exactly those 3 messages, once each, in the 
original order, and A's items become normal sent messages.
242:- AC-003 (FR-001, FR-006, BR-005): Given the browser reports online but A's create request fails with a network 
error after dispatch, then the item becomes unknown, is fenced, and is delivered exactly once without user action once 
requests succeed.
243:- AC-004 (FR-003): Given A has a pending item, when A reloads while offline, then the browser's offline error is 
shown; when the network returns and the app loads again, the item is shown as pending and is delivered exactly once. 
Given A closes the tab with a pending item, then reopening the app later delivers it once.
244:- AC-005 (FR-004, BR-003, BR-004; must-not R1-q16 B): Given A has two tabs and queued m1, m2 offline in 
conversation X in tab 1, then both appear once in tab 2; when both tabs come back online simultaneously and m1's real 
create request is held, then neither tab sends a create request for m2 until m1 is resolved; the network logs of both 
tabs show no overlapping create requests for X; B receives m1 then m2, exactly once each; the database holds one row 
per key.
247:- AC-008 (FR-007, BR-003): Given AC-007's state, when A regains access to X and clicks "Tentar de novo" on m1, 
then m1 and then m2 are delivered in order; given instead A clicks "Apagar" on m1, then m1 is never delivered and m2 
is attempted next.
248:- AC-009 (FR-002, FR-008, BR-005): Given A's create quota is exhausted when A's 12 queued items start draining, 
then rejected items stay pending and retry automatically, and B finally sees all 12 in order, once each. On the real 
route: a replay of an already-stored message and a fence/lookup call do not reduce the remaining create quota but are 
limited by their own budget; concurrent requests for one new key charge the create quota once; concurrent distinct 
keys at the quota boundary store no more messages than the quota allows.
250:- AC-011 (FR-010, BR-002): Given A is offline, when A adds a file, then its upload shows the existing per-file 
error and retry, Send stays disabled, and nothing enters the outbox; given A's uploads succeeded and then the network 
drops, when A sends, then the message stays in the composer with the Phase 4 failure and "Tentar de novo", and nothing 
enters the outbox.
251:- AC-012 (FR-011, FR-012, BR-001, BR-005; must-not R1-q16 A): Given A has unsent items and A's browser session 
ends without Sair (auth cookies/storage cleared or refresh token revoked so the client reports signed out), when C 
signs in on the same browser, then C sees none of A's items and no create or fence request is sent while C is 
authenticated; when A signs in again, A's items (< 24 h) are delivered as A. Given a 401 while A's client session is 
valid, then the item stays pending and is retried.
253:- AC-014 (FR-014): Given the drawer is open, when the browser goes offline, then "Sem conexão — mensagens serão 
enviadas quando voltar" appears immediately; when only Realtime drops for 3 s, no banner appears; when the messages 
stream alone, and separately the conversation-list stream alone, stays unhealthy for more than 5 s, "Reconectando…" 
appears; when all recover, the banner disappears without user action.
255:- AC-016 (FR-016, BR-012; must-not R1-q16 D): Given Realtime is blocked while HTTP works and A's tab is visible, 
when B sends a message at the outage start, then A sees it within 45 s; a message B sends after polling started 
appears within 15 s plus request time; the list's unread counts update. Given only the conversation-list stream is 
unhealthy for more than 30 s, polling starts too. When A's tab is hidden, no polling requests are made; when it 
becomes visible, a fetch happens immediately. When Realtime recovers while a poll is in flight, the message delivered 
by both appears once, and no new polling request starts during the next 60 s.
258:- AC-019 (FR-019, BR-009): With real local Realtime: an anon client, an authenticated non-member, and a client on 
the public topic name receive no typing events of conversation X and cannot send typing events members receive; member 
A receives B's typing; when member E is removed from X, E's app leaves the topic and shows/receives no further typing 
for X (integration + E2E).
260:- AC-021 (FR-021): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices with 6 unread in X, when A reads only the first 
3 on device 1, then within 5 s device 2 shows 3 unread for X; after reading the rest, 0. Given A is the sender of 
messages read by B, then A's receipt summary/details on both of A's sessions match the stored receipts within 5 s.
261:- AC-022 (FR-022): Given healthy connectivity and A on two devices, when A sends on device 1, then it appears once 
on device 2 within 5 s without reload.
263:- AC-024 (FR-023, FR-024, BR-010; must-not R1-q16 F): On the real route and local DB: a message mentioning a 
non-member ID and the sender's own ID stores no mention for either and notifies neither; a message whose text contains 
"@Bruno" typed without selection stores no mention; a forced failure of mention storage stores neither the message nor 
any mention; anon and non-members cannot read mention records; no client can insert/update/delete them directly.
268:- AC-029 (FR-028): Given the metrics route errors, and separately hangs, and separately is flooded with sustained 
traffic, when A sends, goes offline, and reconnects, then AC-001/AC-002 behavior and timing hold, at most one metrics 
request is in flight from A's tab, and no error is shown.
269:- AC-030 (FR-029): Given user E is not a member of X, when `mark_conversation_read` is invoked for E and X through 
every role that can execute it, then the call is refused and E's read state for X is unchanged (integration, local DB).
270:- AC-031 (FR-030): Given the `anon` role, when it calls `get_unread_counts`, then permission is denied; an 
authenticated member still gets correct counts (integration, local DB).
277:- AC-038 (FR-002, FR-006, BR-004): Given A's create request is stored but its response is dropped, then the item 
becomes unknown, the fence resolves it as delivered without a second create, and B sees it once.
279:- AC-040 (FR-001): Given A is online and the create request is held, when A closes the tab before the server 
confirms, then on reopening the app the item is fenced and B sees the message exactly once.
280:- AC-041 (FR-004): Given tab 1 holds the claim and its create request is held, then tab 2 sends no create request 
for that conversation while tab 1's lease is live; when tab 1 is closed (and separately when its lease expires while 
it hangs), tab 2 fences and the message is stored once.
281:- AC-042 (FR-006, FR-009): Given an unknown item whose request was actually stored, when A tries to cancel it or 
its 24 h passes, then no cancel is offered before fencing, the fence resolves it as delivered, and it never shows 
"expirada".
282:- AC-043 (FR-007): Given an item rejected with `CLIENT_MESSAGE_ID_REUSED`, when A clicks "Tentar de novo", then 
its content is stored once under a new key and the conflicting stored message is unchanged.
283:- AC-044 (FR-011, BR-001): Given A's drain is paused before dispatch and the browser's session switches to C (a 
member of the same conversation), when the drain would resume, then no create or fence request is sent as C; a crafted 
create or fence request carrying A as expected sender with C's session is rejected and stores or fences nothing.
284:- AC-045 (FR-006, BR-004): With real local Postgres: given attempt 1's INSERT is blocked (row lock held by the 
test) and the fence for that key runs and reports not stored, when the block is released, then attempt 1 does not 
commit; the item's next attempt (or its cancel/expiry) yields at most one stored message (integration).
285:- AC-046 (FR-007, FR-024, BR-005): On the real route: 404 (missing conversation), 400 (invalid reply target), 413 
(content too large), and 422 are classified definitive in the drawer (failed with text); a 5xx returned after the 
request reached the server is classified unknown and fenced; an unchanged replay after a mentioned recipient left the 
conversation returns the stored message (not 409) and notifies no removed recipient.
286:- AC-047 (FR-011, FR-013): Given a real create response and a real fence response held while the browser switches 
from A to C, and separately while A confirms Sair mesmo, when the responses are released, then no A content appears in 
C's view, no deleted item reappears, and no newer claim is changed.
294:  - Integration (local Supabase Postgres/Realtime and real API routes): sender and attempt fencing (AC-044, 
AC-045), quota charging and replay/fence budget (AC-009), fence/lookup authorization incl. anon and other users' keys 
(AC-038, AC-042, AC-044), outcome classes (AC-046), mention atomicity/validation/replay/RLS (AC-024, AC-046), private 
typing-topic authorization incl. anon/outsider/public topic (AC-019), `mark_conversation_read` membership (AC-030), 
`get_unread_counts` anon revoke (AC-031), metrics route auth, enums, sizes, rate limit, redaction (AC-028).
295:  - E2E (Playwright, real UI; two or more real accounts; two tabs of one account; a second device as a separate 
browser context): every other AC. Network shaping only — the app, routes, and Supabase stay real: browser-context 
offline mode; blocking only the Realtime WebSocket or a single stream; holding a request or response by delayed 
pass-through to the real server; dropping a response or replacing it with a gateway 5xx after pass-through; disabling 
IndexedDB in the page for degraded mode. Expiry may use a test-controlled clock without changing the product rule.
301:- Mandatory failure-path tests: every AC-003..AC-013 and AC-038..AC-050 scenario, short vs long and per-stream 
Realtime drops, hidden-tab polling, polling/Realtime overlap and stale polls, typing tab close and conversation 
switch, typing access by anon/outsider/removed member, forged/plain/changed mentions, visible+hidden tab notification 
race, delayed notification authorization, malicious/oversized/hanging/sustained metrics traffic, restore with lost 
access.

